{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you during your stay. Can you tell me what brought you in today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I just had my gastrostomy tube removed today at the clinic, and since then I’ve been feeling really shaky, dizzy, and I had tingling in my fingers and lips."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And did you eat anything before the procedure?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:08:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I didn’t eat all day. I was nervous about the visit, and I’ve been stressed from a trip I just got back from. I also had diarrhea during the car ride."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. When we checked your blood sugar at the clinic, it was very low—only 35. That explains the dizziness and tingling."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, that’s scary. Is that because I didn’t eat?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Partly, yes. But because of your gastric bypass surgery, your body processes food and sugar differently. Skipping meals can cause your blood sugar to drop quickly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I didn’t realize it could get that low. After they gave me apple juice, I felt better, but then I had cramping and diarrhea again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:13:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s called ‘dumping syndrome.’ When sugar enters your intestine too fast, it can cause cramps, nausea, and diarrhea. We’ll help you avoid that going forward."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. So why am I being admitted?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Because your blood sugar dropped so low and you’ve been having trouble eating, we want to monitor you closely and make sure you’re stable before going home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That sounds good. I do feel better now, but I’m still a little shaky."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve started you on IV fluids with some sugar to help stabilize your levels. We’ll also check your blood sugar regularly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, thank you. Should I be worried about this happening again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll give you a plan to prevent it. You’ll need to eat small, frequent snacks—even when you’re not hungry—especially first thing in the morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. What about my diet? I was on a bariatric diet before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll start you on Stage 1—clear liquids—and slowly advance as you tolerate it. By tomorrow, if all goes well, we’ll move you up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2171-03-28 17:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that sounds manageable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2171-03-29 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How are you feeling today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2171-03-29 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better, I think. I had some broth last night and didn’t feel sick. But I’ve been burping a lot."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2171-03-29 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s common after surgery and with the changes in your digestion. It should improve as you start eating more."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2171-03-29 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, good. My blood sugar was 88 this morning when the nurse checked it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2171-03-29 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a great improvement. We’ll keep monitoring it. We also did an X-ray this morning to make sure everything looks okay in your abdomen."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2171-03-29 08:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What did it show?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2171-03-29 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "There’s a small amount of contrast left from your procedure, but no signs of a blockage or free air. That’s reassuring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2171-03-29 08:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good to hear. Can I move up to a thicker diet today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2171-03-29 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll advance you to Stage 2 today—full liquids like yogurt and pudding. We’ll keep an eye on how you tolerate it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2171-03-30 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. You’re doing well. Blood sugars are stable, and you’ve been tolerating your diet. We’re going to move you to Stage 4 today—soft foods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2171-03-30 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great! I’m actually feeling hungry for the first time in days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2171-03-30 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good sign. Just remember to eat slowly and in small amounts. We also want to talk about your medications."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2171-03-30 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I’ve been taking my nortriptyline and quetiapine. Should I keep doing that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2171-03-30 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue those. We’re also adding pantoprazole twice a day to protect your stomach, since you’ve had ulcers in the past."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2171-03-30 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, and what about pain? I’ve had some mild discomfort at the tube site."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2171-03-30 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We gave IV pain medication yesterday, but now you can take liquid Tylenol if needed. The pain should continue to improve."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2171-03-30 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And no ibuprofen, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2171-03-30 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Avoid NSAIDs like ibuprofen—they can cause ulcers. Stick to Tylenol only."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2171-03-31 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing really well. We’re planning to discharge you today. You’re stable, eating well, and your blood sugar is under control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2171-03-31 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great news. What do I need to do when I get home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2171-03-31 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Stay on the Stage 4 diet, eat small snacks throughout the day, and check your blood sugar regularly—especially if you feel shaky or dizzy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2171-03-31 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if my blood sugar is low?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2171-03-31 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Eat a small snack with some carbs, wait 15 minutes, and check again. We’ve also prescribed promethazine for nausea if it comes back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2171-03-31 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, and when should I call the doctor?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2171-03-31 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "If you have a fever, severe pain, redness or drainage from the site, or can’t keep food down. Otherwise, follow up with your surgeon in a week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2171-03-31 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel much more confident now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2171-03-31 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers ready, and you should be able to go home in a few hours."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2171-03-31 11:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a quick check-in before you leave. Any new symptoms?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2171-03-31 11:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I feel fine. Ate a soft meal and my blood sugar was 92 an hour ago."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2171-03-31 11:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. Remember to crush all your pills so they’re easier to absorb, and start taking the chewable multivitamin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2171-03-31 11:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will do. And no heavy lifting for six weeks, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2171-03-31 11:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Correct. You can walk and do light activity, but avoid anything strenuous, especially involving your abdomen."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2171-03-31 11:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thanks again for all your help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2171-03-31 11:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and we’ll see you at your follow-up."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2171-03-28 17:02:00",
    "admission_end": "2171-03-31 16:05:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted following gastrostomy tube removal due to symptomatic hypoglycemia with blood glucose of 35 mg/dL, likely exacerbated by fasting and her history of Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. She experienced dumping syndrome after oral glucose administration and required IV dextrose and close metabolic monitoring. During her hospitalization, she was advanced from clear liquids to a Stage IV bariatric diet without complications, remained hemodynamically stable, and had no further episodes of hypoglycemia. She was discharged home with instructions on frequent small meals, blood glucose monitoring, wound care, and medication management, including new prescriptions for pantoprazole and promethazine. Follow-up was arranged to monitor for long-term metabolic and gastrointestinal stability.",
    "problems": [
      "Other and unspecified postsurgical nonabsorption",
      "Bariatric surgery status",
      "Need for prophylactic vaccination and inoculation against influenza"
    ]
  }
}
